<article class="article-shell"><header class="article-header"><p class="article-kicker">APPLE WALLET · 7 MIN READ · AUGUST 28, 2026</p><h1>PKPass Signature Errors</h1><p class="article-dek">What to check in the bundle, manifest, hashes, detached signature, and certificate chain before you rebuild everything.</p><div class="article-header-meta"><span>StackHal Field Notes</span><span>Debugging guide</span></div></header><div class="article-layout"><aside class="article-toc" aria-label="On this page"><span>ON THIS PAGE</span><a href="#bundle">Bundle anatomy</a><a href="#manifest">Manifest hashes</a><a href="#signature">The signature</a><a href="#certificates">Certificates</a><a href="#errors">Common errors</a><a href="#checklist">Checklist</a></aside><div class="article-content">
<p id="bundle" class="article-lead">A <code>.pkpass</code> is a ZIP archive with a strict top-level layout. A signature failure is often caused by one changed byte, one nested directory, or one file included in the ZIP that was not represented in the manifest—not by the cryptographic library itself.</p>
<figure class="article-visual pkpass-structure"><div class="package-folder"><b>event.pkpass</b><span>▾</span><code>pass.json</code><code>manifest.json</code><code>signature</code><code>icon.png</code><code>logo.png</code></div><div class="package-flow"><span>files</span><b>SHA-1</b><span>manifest.json</span><b>PKCS#7</b><span>signature</span></div><figcaption>The manifest describes bytes; the detached signature protects the manifest.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="article-callout article-callout-accent"><strong>Debug in this order</strong><span>Structure → manifest hashes → signature bytes → certificate chain → delivery headers. Rebuilding first destroys the evidence that tells you which layer failed.</span></div>
<h2 id="manifest">1. Start with manifest.json and SHA-1</h2><p>The manifest is a JSON object mapping each pass file (apart from the manifest itself and usually the signature) to its SHA-1 digest. Hash the exact bytes stored in the archive. Do not hash a pretty-printed JSON version, decoded image data, or a source file from before the final packaging step.</p><pre class="article-code"><code>{
  "pass.json": "9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015a3bf4f1b",
  "icon.png": "2bb80d537b1da3e38bd30361aa855686bde0ba9a"
}</code></pre><p>One changed newline in <code>pass.json</code>, a recompressed image, or a different case in a filename produces a different digest. A manifest can be valid JSON and still be cryptographically wrong.</p>
<h2 id="signature">2. The signature is detached PKCS#7</h2><p>The <code>signature</code> file is a detached CMS/PKCS#7 signature over the exact bytes of <code>manifest.json</code>. “Detached” means the signed content is not embedded in the signature file; the verifier must load the manifest separately. If you sign a different serialization than the one placed in the ZIP, verification fails.</p><div class="article-screen screen-pkpass" role="img" aria-label="Illustration of StackHal PKPass Inspector showing bundle structure, manifest hashes, and signature status"><div class="screen-chrome"><span class="screen-dot"></span><span class="screen-dot"></span><span class="screen-dot"></span><span>stackhal.com / apple-pkpass-inspector</span></div><div class="pkpass-screen-body"><div class="pkpass-toolbar"><strong>PKPASS INSPECTOR</strong><span class="screen-status">NEEDS REVIEW</span></div><div class="pkpass-grid"><div><small>BUNDLE</small><strong>7 files</strong><em class="screen-good">top-level layout</em></div><div><small>MANIFEST</small><strong>5 / 5 hashes</strong><em class="screen-good">match</em></div><div class="pkpass-warning"><small>SIGNATURE</small><strong>Untrusted chain</strong><em>WWDR certificate missing</em></div></div></div><span class="screen-caption">Inspect the evidence layer by layer before changing the bundle.</span></div>
<h2 id="certificates">3. Check the certificate chain, not only the leaf</h2><p>The signing certificate must be appropriate for pass signing, valid for the signing time, and chained to the Apple Worldwide Developer Relations (WWDR) intermediate and a trusted Apple root. A leaf certificate can be present while the chain is incomplete, expired, revoked, or mismatched with the team identifier in the pass.</p><p>Also check that the signer’s private key was paired with the certificate used to create the signature. A successful local signing command does not prove the recipient can build the same trust chain.</p>
<h2 id="errors">4. Common packaging errors</h2><ul class="article-checklist"><li><strong>Nested ZIP root:</strong> the archive contains <code>event/pass.json</code> instead of <code>pass.json</code> at the top level.</li><li><strong>Stale manifest:</strong> an image or <code>pass.json</code> changed after its hash was recorded.</li><li><strong>Signature included in the manifest:</strong> the verifier cannot resolve a stable self-hash.</li><li><strong>Wrong bytes signed:</strong> the signature covers a different newline, encoding, or JSON serialization.</li><li><strong>Wrong certificate:</strong> the certificate is for another team, environment, or purpose.</li><li><strong>ZIP delivery:</strong> the server sends an HTML error page, modifies content, or uses an unexpected filename/MIME type.</li></ul>
<h2 id="checklist">A pre-rebuild checklist</h2><ol class="article-checklist"><li>Unzip without modifying timestamps or bytes.</li><li>List the archive and confirm all required files are at the root.</li><li>Recalculate every manifest SHA-1 from the archive bytes.</li><li>Verify the signature against the exact manifest file.</li><li>Inspect the full certificate chain and team identifier.</li><li>Download the delivered file again and compare its hash to the original.</li></ol>
<div class="article-cta"><p class="article-kicker">READY TO INSPECT THE EVIDENCE?</p><h2>Open the bundle before rebuilding it.</h2><p>PKPass Inspector checks structure, pass data, manifest hashes, signature metadata, and certificate clues in one readable report.</p><div class="article-cta-actions"><a class="article-button article-button-primary" href="{{ path('pkpass_inspector') }}">Open PKPass Inspector →</a><a class="article-button" href="{{ path('blog_index') }}">Back to the blog</a></div></div>
